System Architectural Styles:
    We are already aware about monolith and microservice

Stateless:
    Every request contains all information needed to process it.
    The server does not remember anything about previous requests.
Example 1:
    REST API
        JWT contains user identity & permissions
        Server does not store session
        Any instance can handle the request

Benefits:
    ✅ Horizontally scalable
    ✅ Easy load balancing
    ✅ Fault tolerant
    ✅ Simple deployments

Stateful:
    The server remembers past interactions (state) and uses it to process future requests.

Example 1:
    Shopping Cart
        Cart is incremental
        Server must remember cart contents

Systems:
    E-commerce carts -> Why?
        Because cart should know the previous data stored in cart.
        The cart data is stateful, not the cart service.
        Yes, by this thinking every database is stateful.
        And that's what we do in hybrid approach externalize the state. So, that overall service can remain
        stateless
    Banking transactions
        As, payment, balance needs to be stored
    Multiplayer games
        Previous position of the player needs to be stored.
    Playback progress ->  Why?
    WebSocket Chat [We also build this, user session or user sate is stored in redis]
        Here redis works as Stateful.
        Server remains stateless, state moved externally or outsource. This is the modern best practice.

   Scalability   -> Harder
   load balancing  -> Sticky sessions
   Fault tolerant   -> Risk of data loss
   Simple deployments

